WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.846
Task statement 2.4.

2
00:00:01.846 --> 00:00:06.026
Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows.

3
00:00:06.026 --> 00:00:15.732
A developer-productivity team wants every engineer to get the same Jira and docs servers on clone, without a single token landing in git.

4
00:00:17.132 --> 00:00:21.429
The Model Context Protocol is a shared plug shape for integrations.

5
00:00:21.429 --> 00:00:26.431
A server offers tools Claude can call, resources Claude can read, and prompts.

6
00:00:26.431 --> 00:00:32.138
Claude Code and the Agent SDK speak the protocol, so a server written once works in both.

7
00:00:32.138 --> 00:00:38.295
Your job as an architect is choosing where each server is configured and how the agent finds it.

8
00:00:39.695 --> 00:00:41.765
Scope decides who gets a server.

9
00:00:41.765 --> 00:00:48.105
A project file named dot mcp dot json lives in the repo and is shared with everyone who clones it.

10
00:00:48.105 --> 00:00:52.634
User scope lives in your home config and follows you to every project.

11
00:00:52.634 --> 00:00:57.292
Keep secrets out of the shared file with environment variable expansion.

12
00:00:57.292 --> 00:01:03.439
Today the add command defaults to local scope, so pass the project scope flag for team servers.

13
00:01:04.839 --> 00:01:06.648
Two things make MCP pay off.

14
00:01:06.648 --> 00:01:07.746
First, resources.

15
00:01:07.746 --> 00:01:15.564
A server can expose a catalog, such as a table list or a document index, that the agent reads instead of exploring blind.

16
00:01:15.564 --> 00:01:16.921
Second, descriptions.

17
00:01:16.921 --> 00:01:23.706
If a powerful search tool has a one-line description, the agent will happily ignore it and grep the repo.

18
00:01:23.706 --> 00:01:29.585
And for standard systems like GitHub, reach for an existing server before writing your own.

19
00:01:30.985 --> 00:01:34.292
Let us apply it to the developer productivity scenario.

20
00:01:34.292 --> 00:01:41.026
The shared Jira server goes in the committed project file, with the token referenced as an environment variable.

21
00:01:41.026 --> 00:01:45.294
Your personal experiment stays in user scope so teammates never see it.

22
00:01:45.294 --> 00:01:47.759
GitHub uses an existing community server.

23
00:01:47.759 --> 00:01:54.793
The one internal system, a code index, gets a custom server with a description good enough that the agent prefers it.

24
00:01:56.193 --> 00:01:59.169
Most traps here are about scope and secrets.

25
00:01:59.169 --> 00:02:01.874
Team servers belong in the project file.

26
00:02:01.874 --> 00:02:04.241
Personal ones belong in user scope.

27
00:02:04.241 --> 00:02:07.893
Tokens never go into git, only variable references do.

28
00:02:07.893 --> 00:02:13.303
And remember the agent only prefers your MCP tool when its description earns it.

29
00:02:14.703 --> 00:02:15.277
To recap.

30
00:02:15.277 --> 00:02:18.214
Choose the scope by who should get the server.

31
00:02:18.214 --> 00:02:21.087
Reference secrets with environment variables.

32
00:02:21.087 --> 00:02:25.300
Use resources to hand the agent a map instead of making it search.

33
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:31.940
Describe tools well so they actually get used, and only build a custom server when no existing one fits.

